];

const faq = [
  { q: 'Are there ravens in the Alps?', a: 'Yes — Common Ravens (Corvus corax) are year-round residents of the European Alps, occurring across all Alpine countries: France, Switzerland, Italy, Austria, Germany (Bavarian Alps), Liechtenstein, and Slovenia. They are found from low valley habitats up to and beyond 3,000m in summer. Alpine ravens exploit a range of food sources including ungulate carrion (chamois, ibex, red deer), refuse at ski resorts and mountain restaurants, and invertebrate prey in summer. Their population across the Alps has been stable to slightly increasing in recent decades as large-ungulate populations have recovered.' },
  { q: 'How high do ravens fly in the Alps?', a: 'Common Ravens have been recorded at considerable altitude in the Alps — the species is documented above 3,000m at several Swiss and Austrian sites, and raven sightings above 4,000m are recorded from Mont Blanc and other high peaks, though these are likely birds in transit or foraging at exceptional altitude rather than regular high-altitude residents. Ravens nest on cliff faces typically between 500m and 2,500m in the Alps, with most nests in the 800–1,800m range. Their large body mass and soaring capability allows them to exploit thermals above mountain ridges, and they have been recorded soaring alongside lammergeiers (bearded vultures) above glaciated terrain.' },
  { q: 'Do ravens follow skiers and tourists in the Alps?', a: 'Yes — Alpine ravens are strongly associated with human activity in mountain environments. At ski resorts throughout the Alps, ravens are regular attendants at outdoor restaurants, rubbish disposal areas, and car parks. They have learned to exploit the food waste associated with ski tourism and are often bold enough to approach tables and steal food from skiers. This human-subsidy effect is well documented: raven densities near ski resorts are significantly higher than in undisturbed alpine terrain at comparable altitude. Ravens at Zermatt, Grindelwald, St Moritz, and other major Alpine resorts are regularly photographed and are locally regarded as a characteristic feature of the ski environment.' },
];
