    <h1>Ravens in the Alps: Ecology, Altitude, and Mountain Life</h1>
  </header>

  <section class="qa-snippet" aria-label="Direct answer">
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Common Ravens are year-round Alpine residents from valley floors to above 3,000m — among the highest-altitude passerines in Europe. They exploit chamois and ibex carrion on high snowfields, nest on limestone cliff faces, and have adapted extensively to ski-resort food subsidies. The Alps support one of the densest raven populations in central Europe, with the species present in all Alpine countries.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Alpine Habitat and Altitude Range</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Ravens occupy a wider altitudinal range in the Alps than almost any other resident bird:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Valley floor to subalpine zone (up to ~1,800m):</strong> mixed habitat — forests, farmland, villages. Ravens nest on cliff faces in valley walls, gorges, and quarries. They exploit agricultural carrion, road kill, and refuse in lower zones.</li>
      <li><strong>Alpine zone (1,800–2,800m):</strong> above the treeline, ravens exploit ungulate carrion (chamois, ibex) on steep rocky terrain. Nests at this altitude are typically on exposed cliff faces above scree and glacier margins.</li>
      <li><strong>Nival zone (above 2,800m):</strong> ravens range into this zone to scavenge on snowfields, particularly around ibex populations and at high-altitude passes. Breeding at this altitude is rare; foraging visits are documented.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>This altitudinal flexibility means Alpine ravens are present year-round even in areas with significant winter snowpack — their large body size and efficient thermoregulation allows them to remain on territory while other corvids descend to lower altitudes.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Food Sources and Ungulate Ecology</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Alpine raven ecology is closely tied to mountain ungulate populations:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup><sup><a href="#src-2">2</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Chamois (Rupicapra rupicapra):</strong> the chamois is the primary wild carrion source for Alpine ravens. Chamois killed by avalanche, falls, or winter starvation represent episodic large carrion events that ravens exploit communally. Ravens have been documented at chamois carcasses within hours of death.</li>
      <li><strong>Alpine ibex (Capra ibex):</strong> reintroduced across the Swiss Alps in the 20th century, ibex populations have increased substantially, providing a larger carrion base. Ravens at Gran Paradiso (Italy) and the Bernese Oberland (Switzerland) have been studied in relation to ibex mortality.</li>
      <li><strong>Red deer and roe deer:</strong> in lower Alpine zones, deer carrion is an important winter food source, particularly in areas with significant hunting. Ravens follow hunting parties and exploit gut piles left at shooting sites.</li>
      <li><strong>Lammergeier association:</strong> the bearded vulture (lammergeier, Gypaetus barbatus), reintroduced to the Alps from 1986, is a food competitor for large carrion. Ravens and lammergeiers co-occur at carcasses, with the lammergeier's bone-feeding specialisation reducing direct competition.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Ski Resorts as Food Subsidies</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> The expansion of Alpine ski tourism since the mid-20th century has created a substantial food subsidy for mountain ravens:<sup><a href="#src-2">2</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li>Mountain restaurants (Bergrestaurants, refuges, rifugi) produce food waste that ravens exploit. Ravens at high-altitude restaurants are often tame enough to take food from tables — a parallel to the coastal situation where ravens exploit fishing harbours.</li>
      <li>Car parks at ski resorts concentrate food waste and represent predictable winter food sources when natural carrion is scarce or inaccessible under deep snow.</li>
      <li>Ravens have been documented opening rubbish bags and stealing from unattended rucksacks at Alpine huts — behaviour requiring problem-solving skills consistent with their cognitive capabilities.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Conservation Status in the Alps</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Ravens were heavily persecuted in the European Alps through the 19th and early 20th centuries — shot as vermin threatening livestock. They were extirpated from large parts of their Alpine range by 1900 and recovered during the mid-20th century as protection improved. Today ravens are legally protected across all Alpine countries under national wildlife legislation and the EU Birds Directive. Population trends in Switzerland, Austria, and France show stable to slightly increasing numbers. The species is not of conservation concern in the Alpine region.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
  </section>

